WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.730
A model for the field-trip budget tells

2
00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:05.390
the faculty to hire two and a half buses.

3
00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:08.650
Rounding up breaks the budget, and

4
00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:10.630
rounding down leaves students behind.

5
00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:12.890
What should the model have said?

6
00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:17.020
Today: why many real decisions need

7
00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:20.040
whole numbers, why rounding a linear

8
00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:23.320
solution can fail badly, and how branch

9
00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:25.620
and bound finds proven optima.

10
00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:28.930
An integer program is a linear

11
00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:31.290
program in which some variables

12
00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:32.610
must be whole numbers.

13
00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:35.250
Yes-or-no decisions become

14
00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:36.710
zero-one variables.

15
00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:39.200
This small change makes the

16
00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:41.160
problem far harder in general.

17
00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:45.150
In the knapsack problem, each item has a

18
00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:48.310
weight and a value, and we choose items to

19
00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:50.470
maximise value within a capacity.

20
00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:53.440
The linear relaxation allows

21
00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:54.700
fractions of items.

22
00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:57.430
Its value is an upper bound

23
00:00:57.430 --> 00:00:58.570
on the true optimum.

24
00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:02.050
Here the relaxation happens to

25
00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:03.670
be whole, giving sixteen.

26
00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:06.380
But change the capacity to seven,

27
00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:09.420
and the relaxation picks a and two

28
00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:11.060
thirds of c, worth fourteen.

29
00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:14.530
Rounding down gives ten, yet b

30
00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:16.130
plus c gives thirteen.

31
00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:20.020
Branch and bound splits on a fractional

32
00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:22.680
variable, solving one branch with it set

33
00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:25.240
to zero and one with it set to one.

34
00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:27.840
Each relaxation gives a bound.

35
00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:31.240
Any branch whose bound is worse than the

36
00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:33.580
best whole solution found so far is

37
00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:34.000
pruned.

38
00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:37.420
Students tend to round linear

39
00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:39.480
solutions or believe the solver

40
00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:40.680
checks every combination.

41
00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:44.120
Draw the branch-and-bound tree for a

42
00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:45.960
four-item knapsack on the board.

43
00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:49.670
Let students compute each bound and

44
00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:51.450
decide which branches to prune.

45
00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:53.060
Quick check!

46
00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:56.200
The relaxation of a maximisation

47
00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:57.740
knapsack gives fourteen.

48
00:01:58.510 --> 00:02:00.130
What does that tell you about

49
00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:01.470
the integer optimum?

50
00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:04.220
It is at most fourteen.

51
00:02:05.030 --> 00:02:08.210
The relaxation has more freedom, so it

52
00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:09.910
can only do as well or better.

53
00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:13.190
Here the true optimum is thirteen.

54
00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:17.080
In the app, open the knapsack lab.

55
00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:20.600
Pick items by hand, compare with

56
00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:23.180
